{0 Call GPT-6, Claude 5.5 and Grok 4.7 from OCaml with Eio}

As of 27 October 2026, install chatoyant with [opam install chatoyant] and use [Chatoyant_native] inside [Eio_main.run] to call GPT-6, Claude 5.5 and Grok 4.7 from OCaml. Create one [Chat] per provider and set [OPENAI_API_KEY], [ANTHROPIC_API_KEY] and [XAI_API_KEY] in the process environment.

This guide targets the API in this repository. Model access depends on your provider account. The examples use explicit model IDs so a preset change cannot silently change their selection.

{1 Install and build}

Install [chatoyant] and [eio_main] with opam: [opam install chatoyant eio_main]. Use OCaml 5.3 or later. Set each API key in the environment of the program; keep secrets out of source files.

Save the OCaml blocks below, in order, as [guide.ml]. Add a [dune] file with [(executable (name guide) (libraries chatoyant eio_main))]. Build with [dune build guide.exe] and run with [dune exec ./guide.exe]. The final entry point makes three paid requests. The other functions are examples you can call explicitly.

The repository keeps the function definitions in [examples/ocaml_llm_guide/guide_steps.ml] and the entry point in [guide_entry.ml]. They are compiled during the default build without executing requests. The entry point is also checked against an explicit Eio runner signature when [eio_main] is unavailable.

{1 A minimal call for each provider}

Use [ask_text] when you only need text. Both the native module and the aggregate [Chatoyant.Chat] expose this API. Each chat retains its conversation history; reuse it for follow-up turns.

{[
module Chat = Chatoyant_native.Chat

let print_text = function
  | Ok text -> print_endline text
  | Error error -> prerr_endline (Chatoyant.Error.provider error)

let openai_call env =
  let chat = Chat.openai ~model:"gpt-6.1-sol" env () in
  Chat.ask_text ~max_tokens:128 "Explain Eio cancellation in one sentence." chat
  |> print_text

let anthropic_call env =
  let chat = Chat.anthropic ~model:"claude-sonnet-5-5" env () in
  Chat.ask_text ~max_tokens:128 "Explain Eio cancellation in one sentence." chat
  |> print_text

let xai_call env =
  let chat = Chat.xai ~model:"grok-4.7" env () in
  Chat.ask_text ~max_tokens:128 "Explain Eio cancellation in one sentence." chat
  |> print_text
]}

OpenAI uses the Responses API. Anthropic uses its Messages API. xAI has its own native provider constructor. [~api_key] can override environment lookup on each constructor.

{1 Model presets}

These are the mappings in {{:https://github.com/Anonyfox/chatoyant/blob/main/lib/core/preset.ml}lib/core/preset.ml}. Presets select model IDs; they are not measured latency or quality guarantees.

{t
| Preset | OpenAI | Anthropic | xAI | Meta |
| fast | gpt-6-luna | claude-haiku-5-5 | grok-4.20-0309-non-reasoning | muse-spark-1.3 |
| cheap | gpt-6-luna | claude-haiku-5-5 | grok-4.20-0309-non-reasoning | muse-spark-1.3 |
| balanced | gpt-6.1-sol | claude-sonnet-5-5 | grok-4.3 | muse-spark-1.3 |
| best | gpt-6-astra | claude-fable-5-1 | grok-4.7 | muse-spark-1.3 |
| reasoning | gpt-6-astra | claude-fable-5-1 | grok-4.7 | muse-spark-1.3 |
}

Resolve presets explicitly before passing [~model]. Local and OpenRouter have no preset mapping. Selecting the [Reasoning] model preset is separate from setting the request's [Options.reasoning] effort.

{[
let balanced_openai env =
  let model =
    Chatoyant.Core.Preset.resolve_model_preset
      ~provider:Chatoyant.Provider.Provider.Openai
      Chatoyant.Core.Preset.Balanced
    |> Option.get
  in
  Chat.openai ~model env ()
]}

{1 Switch providers with the same application code}

Pass a [Chat.t] to application functions. Choose the constructor and model at the boundary. Creating another chat starts a separate history; it does not transfer the old conversation. [Chat.clone] and [Chat.fork] retain the original provider.

{[
let summarize chat text =
  Chat.ask_text ~max_tokens:256 ("Summarize this text:\n" ^ text) chat

let switch_provider env provider text =
  let chat =
    match provider with
    | `Openai -> Chat.openai ~model:"gpt-6.1-sol" env ()
    | `Anthropic -> Chat.anthropic ~model:"claude-sonnet-5-5" env ()
    | `Xai -> Chat.xai ~model:"grok-4.7" env ()
  in
  summarize chat text
]}

For independent requests with a reusable client, [Chatoyant.gen_text] forks the supplied client before appending messages. Direct [Chat.ask_text] calls mutate history.

{1 Streaming: what the native API supports}

[Chat.stream_accumulate] consumes a list of already decoded frames. It does not open an HTTP stream or deliver tokens as they arrive. [Chat.generate] and [Chat.ask_text] return after generation. The native HTTP effect buffers response bodies, so adding a stream flag does not provide live token delivery.

For a custom transport, decode provider SSE events with [Chatoyant.Provider.Openai.responses_stream_events_of_chunks], [Chatoyant.Provider.Anthropic.stream_events_of_chunks] or [Chatoyant.Provider.Xai.responses_stream_events_of_chunks]. Map those events to [Core.Stream.frame] values before accumulation. A UI that requires live updates needs a transport that exposes chunks and incremental event decoding.

The following function accepts those normalized frames, prints their text deltas and records the accumulated result in the chat. Its input is already buffered; it makes no network request.

{[
let accumulate_stream chat (frames : Chatoyant.Core.Stream.frame list) =
  List.iter
    (fun (frame : Chatoyant.Core.Stream.frame) ->
      Option.iter
        (fun text ->
          print_string text;
          flush stdout)
        frame.content_delta)
    frames;
  Chat.stream_accumulate frames chat
]}

{1 Let the model call a tool}

A typed tool carries argument and result schemas. The SDK decodes model arguments before calling the handler and sends the result back into its generation loop. This example computes a square locally. It does not grant access to external systems. Tool selection remains the model's decision.

{[
type square_args = { value : int }

let square_args_codec =
  let open Chatoyant.Schema.Codec in
  object1
    (field "value" (int ()) ~get:(fun args -> args.value))
    (fun value -> { value })

let square_tool =
  Chatoyant.Core.Tool.create_typed ~name:"square"
    ~description:"Square an integer." ~args:square_args_codec
    ~result:(Chatoyant.Schema.Codec.int ()) (fun _context args ->
      Ok (args.value * args.value))

let tool_call env =
  let chat = Chat.openai ~model:"gpt-6.1-sol" env () in
  Chat.ask_text ~tools:[ square_tool ] ~max_tokens:256
    "Use square to calculate the square of 12." chat
]}

Use bounded inputs for arithmetic in production. The default tool loop allows 50 iterations; [Options.max_tool_iterations] changes that bound. Handlers return [Error message] for application failures.

{1 Structured output with a schema}

A codec defines the JSON Schema and the OCaml decoder together. This schema requires an object with a string [language] and a boolean [uses_eio]. [gen_data] returns the decoded record or a provider error.

{[
type project = { language : string; uses_eio : bool }

let project_codec =
  let open Chatoyant.Schema.Codec in
  object2
    (field "language" (string ()) ~get:(fun p -> p.language))
    (field "uses_eio" (bool ()) ~get:(fun p -> p.uses_eio))
    (fun language uses_eio -> { language; uses_eio })

let structured_output env =
  let client = Chatoyant.openai ~model:"gpt-6.1-sol" env in
  Chatoyant.gen_data ~name:"project" ~strict:true ~codec:project_codec
    ~max_tokens:256 client "Describe an OCaml project using Eio."
]}

OpenAI sends Responses structured output settings. OpenAI-compatible providers use [response_format]. Anthropic receives a schema instruction with local validation. A schema does not guarantee factual accuracy. [chatoyant.ppx] can derive codecs for larger record types.

{1 Tokens and cost for a request}

Use [ask] instead of [ask_text] to retain metadata. [usage_source] distinguishes provider-reported usage from estimates. [cost.actual_usd] is optional; do not replace a missing billed amount with a claim that the estimate is the invoice.

{[
let request_accounting env =
  let chat = Chat.openai ~model:"gpt-6.1-sol" env () in
  match Chat.ask ~max_tokens:128 "What is an Eio switch?" chat with
  | Error error -> prerr_endline (Chatoyant.Error.provider error)
  | Ok result ->
      Printf.printf
        "%s\ninput=%d output=%d cached=%d source=%s estimated=$%.6f\n"
        result.content result.usage.input_tokens result.usage.output_tokens
        result.usage.cached_tokens
        (Chatoyant.Tokens.Cost.source_to_string result.usage_source)
        result.cost.estimated_usd;
      Option.iter (Printf.printf "provider cost=$%.6f\n") result.cost.actual_usd
]}

These USD rates per million tokens are the rows in {{:https://github.com/Anonyfox/chatoyant/blob/main/lib/tokens/pricing.ml}lib/tokens/pricing.ml}. They are the SDK's accounting inputs as of this guide's date.

{t
| Model | Input | Output | Cached input | Cache write 5m | Cache write 1h |
| gpt-6.1-sol | 2.00 | 10.00 | 0.10 | unavailable | unavailable |
| claude-sonnet-5-5 | 2.00 | 10.00 | 0.10 | 2.50 | 4.00 |
| grok-4.7 | 2.00 | 6.00 | 0.50 | unavailable | unavailable |
}

For 1,000 uncached input tokens and 200 output tokens, these rows give $0.004 for GPT-6.1 Sol or Claude Sonnet 5.5 and $0.0032 for Grok 4.7. This arithmetic excludes caching and additional charges. OpenAI input above 272K uses a higher tier that this table does not model. Pricing lookup can fall back to family estimates for unknown IDs; use exact IDs when auditing costs.

{1 Pin the Anthropic API version}

The default header is [anthropic-version: 2023-06-01]. Pass [?anthropic_version] to the Anthropic constructor to pin it explicitly. This header is separate from the Claude model ID. [~beta_headers] accepts optional beta feature headers.

{[
let pinned_anthropic env =
  Chat.anthropic ~model:"claude-sonnet-5-5" ~anthropic_version:"2023-06-01" env
    ()
]}

{1 Errors and missing keys}

Constructors accept omitted keys. A missing key produces [Missing_api_key] on generation rather than raising at construction. Explicit [~api_key] takes precedence over environment lookup. Treat keys as secrets when logging diagnostics.

{[
let handle_text_result = function
  | Ok text -> print_endline text
  | Error (Chatoyant.Provider.Provider.Missing_api_key { env_key; _ }) ->
      Printf.eprintf "Set %s before generating.\n" env_key
  | Error (Chatoyant.Provider.Provider.Http_error { status; _ }) ->
      Printf.eprintf "Provider returned HTTP %d.\n" status
  | Error error -> prerr_endline (Chatoyant.Error.provider error)
]}

Other variants include [Decode_error], [Unsupported] and [Runtime_error]. Inspect the error before retrying. An HTTP error retains its status and body. Transport failures are surfaced through provider errors; [Error.http] renders errors from direct native HTTP helper calls. Eio cancellation and exceptions from application code still need application-level handling.

{1 Run the minimal calls}

Place this entry point after the definitions above. It calls the providers sequentially. Avoid sharing a mutable chat between concurrent fibers without coordinating access.

{[
let () =
  Eio_main.run (fun env ->
      openai_call env;
      anthropic_call env;
      xai_call env)
]}

{1 Related documentation}

Use {!module:Chatoyant_native} for the complete Eio API and {{!module:Chatoyant}Chatoyant aggregate API} for the aggregate entry point. The same SDK ships as a zero-dependency npm package; see {{:https://github.com/Anonyfox/chatoyant/blob/main/JAVASCRIPT.md}JAVASCRIPT.md} for JavaScript and TypeScript usage.
